<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fujiwara no Michinaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/966">966</a>–1028</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Mido Kampaku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fujiwara no Michinaga was a Japanese statesman under whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>'s control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and its politics reached its zenith. A son of the regent Kaneie, he outmanoeuvred his nephew Korechika after his elder brothers died in 995, becoming Minister of the Right that year and Minister of the Left in 996. Called the Mido <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">Kampaku</a>, regent in all but name, he married four daughters to emperors and future emperors, among them Ichijō, Sanjō, Go-Ichijō and Go-Suzaku. He pressed Sanjō to retire in 1016 and ruled as regent for the young Go-Ichijō, then handed the office to his son Yorimichi. He became a monk in 1019, and his diary records the Heian court at the height of Fujiwara power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-michinaga.jpg" alt="Fujiwara no Michinaga" width="330" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fujiwara_no_Michinaga_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Heian Japan the Fujiwara family dominated the court by marrying its daughters to emperors, and in 1016 <b>Fujiwara no Michinaga</b> became regent for his grandson Emperor Go-Ichijō; later the daimyō, warrior lords with their own samurai, held real power.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Murasaki Shikibu lived at the height of the Fujiwara clan's power, when <b>Fujiwara no Michinaga</b> was the regent in all but name and the most significant political figure of his day; she is believed to have formed the character of Genji partly through her experience of him.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fujiwara no Michinaga</b> could enthrone and dethrone emperors at will.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
